

Patent Claims — Kinetic‑Cryptographic Fluid Matrix (KCFM‑v1.0)

Claim 1 — Core System Claim (Independent Claim)

A system for generating tamper‑proof hydraulic telemetry comprising:

a fluid‑dynamics computation module configured to solve Navier–Stokes and Darcy flow equations under time‑varying boundary conditions;

a tensorization module configured to discretize fluid‑state variables including pressure, velocity components, and turbulence scalars into a spatiotemporal tensor;

a vectorization module configured to flatten said tensor into a state vector representing the complete hydraulic configuration at a given timestep;

an entropy‑derivation module configured to compute a kinetic‑entropy value from gradients of the velocity field;

a key‑generation module configured to derive a cryptographic key from the kinetic‑entropy value and the state vector;

an authenticated‑encryption module configured to encrypt the state vector using the cryptographic key; and

a verification module configured to enforce a mass‑conservation invariant and compute cryptographic integrity hashes,wherein the encrypted state vector forms an immutable, tamper‑evident record of hydraulic system state.

Claim 2 — Mass‑Conservation Verification (Dependent Claim)

The system of Claim 1,wherein the verification module enforces a mass‑conservation constraint[ |\Delta Q| < 10^{-7}, ]such that any violation triggers a numerical‑integrity or physical‑integrity fault condition.

Claim 3 — Kinetic‑Entropy Key Generation (Dependent Claim)

The system of Claim 1,wherein the cryptographic key is derived using a key‑derivation function applied to:

the kinetic‑entropy value computed from the velocity‑gradient field, and

the flattened state vector,thereby binding cryptographic entropy to physical hydraulic dynamics.

Claim 4 — Authenticated Encryption Mesh (Dependent Claim)

The system of Claim 1,wherein the authenticated‑encryption module comprises an AES‑GCM‑SIV cipher and optionally a lattice‑based post‑quantum cryptographic layer.

Claim 5 — Cryptophysical Ledger (Dependent Claim)

The system of Claim 1,wherein encrypted state vectors, mass‑conservation values, and cryptographic integrity hashes are stored in an append‑only cryptophysical ledger enabling forensic reconstruction of hydraulic system behavior.

Claim 6 — Real‑Time Compute‑to‑Encrypt Cycle (Dependent Claim)

The system of Claim 1,wherein the tensorization, entropy derivation, key generation, and encryption occur within a zero‑latency compute‑to‑encrypt cycle suitable for real‑time infrastructure monitoring.

Claim 7 — Tamper‑Proof Telemetry (Independent Claim)

A method for producing tamper‑proof hydraulic telemetry comprising:

computing fluid‑state variables using deterministic fluid‑dynamics equations;

discretizing said variables into a tensor;

flattening the tensor into a state vector;

computing kinetic entropy from the velocity‑gradient field;

deriving a cryptographic key from the kinetic entropy and the state vector;

encrypting the state vector using authenticated encryption;

verifying mass conservation and cryptographic integrity;

storing the encrypted state vector and verification metadata in an immutable record.

Claim 8 — Physical‑Entropy‑Driven Cryptography (Dependent Claim)

The method of Claim 7,wherein cryptographic keys are deterministically derived from physically measured or simulated hydraulic entropy, thereby preventing key reuse and enabling physically grounded cryptographic uniqueness.

Claim 9 — Replay‑Attack Immunity (Dependent Claim)

The method of Claim 7,wherein each encrypted state vector is uniquely bound to its corresponding kinetic‑entropy signature, preventing replay of previously recorded states.

Claim 10 — Infrastructure Integrity Engine (Independent Claim)

An infrastructure‑integrity engine comprising:

a physical solver;

a cryptographic subsystem;

a verification subsystem;

and a cryptophysical ledger,wherein physical invariants and cryptographic invariants jointly define system truth, enabling absolute state verification of hydraulic infrastructure.

